    "result_text": "The build is green: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0** \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule \"reject creating an owner whose lower-cased email is already used by any other owner, responding with 409\", following the codebase's existing duplicate-telephone pattern:\n\n1. **`DuplicateOwnerEmailException`** (new, `rest/advice/`) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending email, mirroring `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException`.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` for the new exception that returns **409 Conflict** as a `ProblemDetail` (with `errors: [\"email\"]`), plus a matching `ERROR_DUPLICATE_EMAIL` message constant.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 after the email is normalized to lower-case (via the existing `normalizeEmail`), it now calls a new `rejectDuplicateEmail(...)` guard that scans existing owners and throws the conflict if any other owner already uses that email (case-insensitive comparison). Owners with no email supplied are skipped, since there's nothing to collide with.\n\nThe check is placed after email normalization so it compares canonical lower-cased values, consistent with how the telephone uniqueness check works against stored E.164 values.",
